        "id": 1942645
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-03T19:11:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What do you mean by re-derive the shared key externally? The shared key is always opaque, so the key material is not visible to the service clients. It could be persisted as a wrapped key, but that can be controlled by the policy (if the key can be wrapped); but depending on your level of trust on the device, that might require that the other party also sends a policy (which `secureKeyImport` can support). Is this what you mean?",
      "parentUuid": "229c47b8_457c1188",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 102,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 103,
        "endChar": 85
      },
      "revId": "37093bf2b94309d3d3c1837e911b5f577b028480",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a5d2d7f7_0b5ad9c9",
        "filename": "staging/security/see/hwcrypto/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/hwcrypto/IOpaqueKey.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 103,
      "author": {
        "id": 2016000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-03T22:06:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I was meaning something different, so I\u0027ll try to explain it more clearly. My thought is that the client could break the opaque nature of the key derived by `calculateSharedKey` (assuming ECDH-based KEM) using `calculateSharedSecret`, which returns arbitrary ECDH calculations to the client, then applying the same derivation parameters to retrieve the derived key in the clear. You could presumably use a similar process to break `secureKeyImport`. Should there be a way to restrict use of `calculateSharedSecret` for some keys to prevent the client from re-deriving opaque keys?",
      "parentUuid": "595838bd_8021c2e0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 102,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 103,
        "endChar": 85
      },
      "revId": "37093bf2b94309d3d3c1837e911b5f577b028480",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7942d012_08e329a9",
        "filename": "staging/security/see/hwcrypto/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/hwcrypto/IOpaqueKey.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 103,
      "author": {
        "id": 1942645
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-05T22:48:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see. Yes, I am planning to have the functions that specifically derive keys to require its policy KeyUse and KeyPermissions to be restrictive so the keys cannot be used for other operations (keyUse::KEY_EXCHANGE and keyPermissions::KEY_MANAGEMENT_KEY are the 2 attributes that I am thinking on using for this). Additionally, the other generic functions (`calculateSharedSecret`) should not accept using keys whose use include KEY_EXCHANGE.",
      "parentUuid": "a5d2d7f7_0b5ad9c9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 102,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 103,
        "endChar": 85
      },
      "revId": "37093bf2b94309d3d3c1837e911b5f577b028480",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "814c6f65_f4b5060a",
        "filename": "staging/security/see/hwcrypto/aidl/android/hardware/security/see/hwcrypto/IOpaqueKey.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 103,
      "author": {
        "id": 2016000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-10T20:12:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Cool, it sounds like the opaque and generic operations would be mutually exclusive.",
      "parentUuid": "7942d012_08e329a9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 102,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 103,
        "endChar": 85
      },
      "revId": "37093bf2b94309d3d3c1837e911b5f577b028480",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
